<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khufu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">26th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a> · Also known as Cheops</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khufu was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Fourth Dynasty in the 26th century BCE and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>. He built the Great Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, the largest of the Egyptian pyramids and one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>. Papyri found at Wadi al-Jarf record the transport of limestone for its construction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khufu.jpg" alt="Khufu" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UserPpPp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Khufu_in_the_Cairo_Egyptian_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khufu was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Fourth Dynasty, in the early Old Kingdom. His name, Khnum-khufu, means Khnum protects me and was dedicated to the creator god Khnum. Egyptologists believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> was his father, but only because later historians handed down that the eldest son or a chosen descendant inherited the throne. The tomb of queen Hetepheres I, found in 1925 east of Khufu's pyramid, gives her the title mother of a king beside the name of Sneferu. Some doubt that she was Sneferu's wife, since she is not known to have borne the title king's wife.</p><p>Khufu's family was large. His wives were Meritites I and Henutsen, and his sons included Kawab, most likely the eldest and crown prince, who died before him, then Djedefre, who became the first successor, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>, who probably followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>It is unclear how long Khufu ruled. Inscriptions from his last years suggest that he was approaching his 30-year jubilee but may have just missed it. A rock inscription at the Dakhla Oasis dates to his twenty-seventh year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments known as the Diary of Merer, found at his harbour at Wadi al-Jarf, log the transport of limestone from Tura to the Great Pyramid in the year after the thirteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> count. Few other details of his political activity are recorded. His name appears in inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elkab">Elkab</a> and Elephantine and in the quarries of Hatnub and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a>.</p><p>He built his pyramid complex on the north-eastern part of the plateau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, at the high end of a natural plateau so that it would be widely visible, and named it Akhet-Khufu, horizon of Khufu. Sediment cores suggest that a harbour connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> worked at Giza in his time and that it carried stones and supplies for the complex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The latest known date of his reign comes from his funeral. Graffiti in a rock-cut pit beside the Great Pyramid record the 28th or 29th year. The only almost completely preserved image of Khufu is a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> figurine, found by Flinders Petrie at Abydos in 1903, whose head was recovered three weeks after the body.</p><p>His mortuary cult was extensive in the Old Kingdom, with at least 67 attested priests by the end of the Sixth Dynasty, and it was reorganised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> depicts him as a cruel tyrant who closed the temples and forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> to work for him. Modern Egyptologists point to the town of Menat-Khufu, nurse of Khufu, and to the long-lasting cult as evidence that Egyptians themselves held a positive view for centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khufu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fourth Dynasty (2613–2494 BCE) was the zenith of royal power: Sneferu, then his son <b>Khufu</b> (2589–2566 BCE), whose sons Djedefre and Khafre may have quarrelled, then Menkaure, Shepseskaf and perhaps Djedefptah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rolls of papyrus found at Wadi al-Jarf in 2013, the Diary of Merer, record a supervisor’s deliveries of white limestone from Tura to the pyramid in the 27th year of <b>Khufu</b>’s reign, naming it dozens of times by its original name, Akhet Khufu.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The archaeological evidence suggests it was created between 2600 and 2500 BC for the king <b>Khufu</b>, builder of the Great Pyramid, or his son Khafre, builder of the second pyramid.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-sphinx-of-giza">Great Sphinx of Giza · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
